cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "transport-godot-mcp: an in-editor MCP server for the Godot editor"
abstract: >
  Drive the Godot editor from an MCP client. A transport layer for an authoring
  client, and the Godot-side counterpart to Unity-MCP, so the Godot host can be
  exercised the same way as the Unity host for the two-implementation interop check.
  It runs two halves at once on purpose: the editor plugin answers what the scene was
  authored as, and MCPRuntime, autoloaded into the running game, answers what the
  scene actually is. Those are different questions, and only the second one can say
  where an object ended up.
authors:
  - family-names: Lee
    given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    email: ernest.lee@chibifire.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
version: 0.1.0-dev.1
date-released: "2026-08-17"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/transport-godot-mcp"
keywords:
  - godot
  - model-context-protocol
  - authoring
  - editor-tooling
references:

  - type: software
    title: "Unity-MCP (com.ivanmurzak.unity.mcp)"
    authors:
      - family-names: Murzak
        given-names: Ivan
    url: "https://github.com/IvanMurzak/Unity-MCP"
    notes: >
      The implementation this is the counterpart to. Having two hosts answer the same
      MCP surface is what makes the interop check a comparison rather than a
      self-report, so this is a design dependency and not merely prior art.

  - type: software
    title: "Model Context Protocol"
    authors:
      - name: Anthropic
    url: "https://modelcontextprotocol.io/"
    notes: "The protocol both halves speak, over HTTP on 8788 for the editor and 8789 for the runtime."

  - type: software
    title: "Godot Engine"
    authors:
      - name: "Godot Engine contributors"
    url: "https://godotengine.org/"
    notes: >
      The host. Its addon discovery rule is why this repository has an addon-root
      branch: Godot only scans res://addons/*/plugin.cfg, so subtreeing the repository
      whole puts plugin.cfg one level too deep and the editor never finds it.
